1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.450
[S01] You need to be extreme if you want your life to change.

2
00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:09.019
[S01] And the best way I can illustrate this is by talking about the story of my birthday in 2021,

3
00:00:09.039 --> 00:00:11.119
[S01] because I remember it vividly.

4
00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:14.179
[S01] This was the time when my business was actually starting to do well.

5
00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:18.750
[S01] I was also in the middle of losing weight because it happened after COVID.

6
00:00:18.769 --> 00:00:20.960
[S01] So I was 20 to 30 pounds overweight.

7
00:00:20.989 --> 00:00:22.629
[S01] I'd been in the gym my entire life.

8
00:00:22.649 --> 00:00:24.059
[S01] I didn't like how I looked.

9
00:00:24.089 --> 00:00:28.239
[S01] This was also a time when I was in the middle of the first draft of my first book,

10
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.210
[S01] The Art of Focus.

11
00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:34.109
[S01] And the weird thing about this is so many things were going well.

12
00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:40.250
[S01] And it felt like at that time, every single weekend, there was a reason to go out

13
00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.609
[S01] and do something that would ruin my progress.

14
00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:47.369
[S01] Things like concerts or hanging out with friends or going out to drink, that kind of stuff.

15
00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:49.679
[S01] Just normal life for normal people.

16
00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:53.420
[S01] And since I was making such good progress, I figured I could take a break.

17
00:00:53.439 --> 00:00:54.350
[S01] So I did.

18
00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:58.670
[S01] I went out, I partied, I drank, I stayed up late, I went off my diet.

19
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:03.159
[S01] And as you could guess, my progress didn't only slow down,

20
00:01:03.189 --> 00:01:05.719
[S01] it just started going downhill rapidly.

21
00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:11.010
[S01] Every weekend, it would take maybe four to five days for my energy and my mental capacity

22
00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:11.890
[S01] to recover.

23
00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.709
[S01] And by that time, there was another event to attend.

24
00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:18.239
[S01] There was another thing to do that would slow down my progress.

25
00:01:18.269 --> 00:01:21.780
[S01] And because of this, I couldn't think straight for writing the draft of a book.

26
00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:25.959
[S01] And obviously, my fitness habits fell off and my business started to slow down because

27
00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.040
[S01] of those two things.

28
00:01:27.069 --> 00:01:31.439
[S01] Now, that felt painful, but the pain wasn't excruciating just yet.

29
00:01:31.469 --> 00:01:33.290
[S01] I didn't have a reason to change.

30
00:01:33.319 --> 00:01:37.890
[S01] Now, for months prior to this, my friend group and I had been planning to go to Seattle

31
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:38.709
[S01] for my birthday.

32
00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:42.810
[S01] And I was already tired of not making progress, but it was my birthday, right?

33
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:47.750
[S01] So I could justify going out and having a little fun one last time.

34
00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:52.379
[S01] So after a weekend of going out and doing the same thing and making very little progress,

35
00:01:52.409 --> 00:01:53.510
[S01] I was done.

36
00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:56.780
[S01] At that point, I was just so fucking mad at myself.

37
00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:00.750
[S01] Because when you have goals that are the most important thing in your world,

38
00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:04.090
[S01] they shine a light on everything you're doing wrong.

39
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:08.889
[S01] The most painful thing in the world is watching those goals fade into the background

40
00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:12.300
[S01] as they're being replaced by goals of shallow pleasure.

41
00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.860
[S01] So the second I got back home, I just disappeared.

42
00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:17.289
[S01] I didn't respond to anyone.

43
00:02:17.319 --> 00:02:18.719
[S01] I became unavailable.

44
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:23.219
[S01] Every invitation or event in any form was just an automatic no.

45
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.009
[S01] I didn't even have to think twice about it.

46
00:02:25.039 --> 00:02:29.819
[S01] And because of that, every single action I took felt seamless.

47
00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:31.729
[S01] I wanted to be disciplined.

48
00:02:31.759 --> 00:02:37.469
[S01] It gave me joy and slowly my mind returned, my body returned, my business returned.

49
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.750
[S01] My entire world changed in an instant.

50
00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.310
[S01] Now, why is that?

51
00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:46.219
[S01] The first reason is because being extreme changes your brain.

52
00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:51.610
[S01] There's a famous saying that summarizes a neuropsychological theory related to neuroplasticity

53
00:02:51.639 --> 00:02:52.659
[S01] called Hebb's Law.

54
00:02:52.689 --> 00:02:57.509
[S01] And in a nutshell, it just says neurons that fire together, wire together.

55
00:02:57.539 --> 00:03:02.479
[S01] So neuroplasticity, as you may know, is your brain's ability to rewire itself

56
00:03:02.509 --> 00:03:05.250
[S01] by forming new neural connections throughout life.

57
00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.780
[S01] Your brain isn't fixed and rigid.

58
00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:13.009
[S01] It's a dynamic network that can adapt, learn, and change based on your experiences,

59
00:03:13.039 --> 00:03:14.509
[S01] thoughts, and actions.

60
00:03:14.539 --> 00:03:21.509
[S01] So being extreme about changing your life helps quicken the process of neuroplasticity

61
00:03:21.539 --> 00:03:22.710
[S01] or changing your brain.

62
00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:28.689
[S01] People scream about how consistency is key, which is true, due to repetition reinforcing

63
00:03:28.719 --> 00:03:29.750
[S01] neural pathways.

64
00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.810
[S01] But we can take it a step further than that.

65
00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:36.150
[S01] Novelty and challenge stimulate neuroplasticity even more.

66
00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.810
[S01] So when you flip the switch and pursue a goal with all your might,

67
00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.319
[S01] you put your brain in an environment that quickly adapts and helps that become your

68
00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:45.219
[S01] new standard.

69
00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:50.719
[S01] The second thing is that intensity and obsession create this neurochemical cocktail in your

70
00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:51.250
[S01] brain.

71
00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.509
[S01] Most people fall into a rut because they seek extrinsic motivators.

72
00:03:55.539 --> 00:04:00.379
[S01] But when you are obsessed, in the context of this discussion, with a goal,

73
00:04:00.409 --> 00:04:05.210
[S01] you are fueled by intrinsic motivators, each of which stack and strengthen each other

74
00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:09.780
[S01] in a way that sustains some degree of flow, which is the state of optimal experience

75
00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.710
[S01] or one of the most enjoyable states of mind.

76
00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.060
[S01] Now, there are five of these intrinsic motivators.

77
00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:18.850
[S01] The first is curiosity, which is the desire to explore the unknown,

78
00:04:18.879 --> 00:04:21.920
[S01] learn how to change, and fill knowledge gaps.

79
00:04:21.949 --> 00:04:25.889
[S01] And this results in good dopamine from novelty and norepinephrine,

80
00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.750
[S01] which heightens attention, preparing you to learn.

81
00:04:28.769 --> 00:04:33.120
[S01] The second intrinsic motivator is passion, where an intense enthusiasm is built for

82
00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:35.639
[S01] the path that allows you to change your life.

83
00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.639
[S01] This results in more good dopamine in norepinephrine.

84
00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:43.180
[S01] The third intrinsic motivator is purpose, which is the feeling that your actions contribute

85
00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:45.079
[S01] to something larger than yourself.

86
00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:49.019
[S01] Achieving goals here results in more dopamine, which reinforces behavior,

87
00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:52.100
[S01] and serotonin stems from significance and belonging.

88
00:04:52.129 --> 00:04:54.420
[S01] Oxytocin stems from connection.

89
00:04:54.449 --> 00:04:59.060
[S01] The fourth intrinsic motivator is autonomy, which is the desire to direct your own life

90
00:04:59.079 --> 00:05:02.680
[S01] and work, to control your choices, actions, and environment.

91
00:05:02.709 --> 00:05:06.810
[S01] And this results in, yet again, more dopamine and a reduction in cortisol,

92
00:05:06.829 --> 00:05:11.250
[S01] which is the stress from feeling put in a box, allowing for creative decision making.

93
00:05:11.279 --> 00:05:15.720
[S01] And the fifth intrinsic motivator is mastery, which is the process of learning and growing

94
00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.019
[S01] being its own reward.

95
00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:20.750
[S01] And this results in sustainable good dopamine that keeps you in the game.

96
00:05:20.779 --> 00:05:24.750
[S01] Those are the intrinsic motivators that create obsession.

97
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.180
[S02] Wired for survival.

98
00:06:01.209 --> 00:06:05.790
[S02] But the interesting thing about being human is that we don't only try to protect and reproduce

99
00:06:05.819 --> 00:06:09.629
[S02] the information in our genes or in our DNA like animals.

100
00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:15.120
[S02] We do that, but we also attempt to protect and reproduce the information in our consciousness

101
00:06:15.149 --> 00:06:18.089
[S02] or our worldview or our concept of self.

102
00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:23.220
[S02] So what that means is that we feel threatened when our body is threatened, obviously, like

103
00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:26.459
[S02] animals, but also when our identity is threatened.

104
00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:30.990
[S02] So on the negative end, we notice information that reinforces our beliefs and worldview,

105
00:06:31.019 --> 00:06:35.189
[S02] often leading to us lashing out at those who question our religious or political beliefs.

106
00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:40.110
[S02] Our identity feels threatened and the brain's response sends that signal, so we feel the

107
00:06:40.139 --> 00:06:41.459
[S02] need to survive.

108
00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:46.319
[S02] Now, on the positive end, when you are identified with a goal that you are obsessed with, your

109
00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:52.829
[S02] mind will automatically notice opportunities in your environment to reinforce that higher

110
00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:54.810
[S02] version of yourself that you were becoming.

111
00:06:54.839 --> 00:06:59.879
[S02] An example of this is if you've ever set out to achieve a goal, like a New Year's resolution,

112
00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:03.720
[S02] and then you start reading a book, and now you're learning things that you didn't even

113
00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:04.829
[S02] know existed before.

114
00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:08.040
[S02] You're noticing diets to try and exercises to try.

115
00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:12.480
[S02] And when losing weight or going to the gym wasn't a priority to you before, you didn't

116
00:07:12.509 --> 00:07:14.970
[S02] care about those things, they didn't even register for you.

117
00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:18.949
[S02] It's the same phenomenon when you hear a word for the first time and then you hear

118
00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:23.670
[S02] it everywhere, or when you see a new car for the first time and then you see it everywhere.

119
00:07:23.699 --> 00:07:29.550
[S02] The point is that if you aren't obsessed with a goal, you are missing out on so much

120
00:07:29.579 --> 00:07:33.420
[S02] in life and so many opportunities where you're wondering, oh, how do I do this?

121
00:07:33.449 --> 00:07:34.259
[S02] How do I do that?

122
00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.269
[S02] Oh, it's it's all luck.

123
00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:40.259
[S02] No, you're just not obsessed with a goal, the goal that allows you to notice those

124
00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:40.740
[S02] things.

125
00:07:40.769 --> 00:07:44.610
[S02] The people who are obsessed with their goals are living in a completely different mind

126
00:07:44.639 --> 00:07:48.779
[S02] space than you, and they don't have trouble succeeding because it's automatic.

127
00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:52.529
[S02] We're wired this way because our ancestors were wired this way.

128
00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:57.870
[S02] When our ancestors were hunting for something or they needed food, their attention was

129
00:07:57.899 --> 00:08:02.250
[S02] heightened and they would notice things in the environment that allowed them to survive.

130
00:08:02.279 --> 00:08:06.750
[S02] Now, if we look to something like cybernetics and apply that to our mind or the psyche,

131
00:08:06.779 --> 00:08:11.399
[S02] we can see that our mind has some form of a homing mechanism toward our goals.

132
00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:16.019
[S02] Our mind accepts information that helps it and rejects information that doesn't.

133
00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:19.350
[S02] You are steered toward your goals without much effort.

134
00:08:19.379 --> 00:08:23.879
[S02] So in summary, if you are obsessed with a goal, the universe will conspire in your

135
00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:26.970
[S02] favor by giving you the resources to achieve it.

136
00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:32.070
[S02] So we understand that being extreme or being obsessed is the best way to change your life.

137
00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:33.539
[S02] But how do you get to that point?

138
00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:33.779
[S02] Right?

139
00:08:33.809 --> 00:08:36.929
[S02] You're you're hearing me say, oh, you need to be obsessed with a goal, but what if you

140
00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:38.220
[S02] weren't obsessed with any goal?

141
00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:39.990
[S02] What if you don't care about anything?

142
00:08:40.019 --> 00:08:41.250
[S02] What if you don't know what to do?

143
00:08:41.279 --> 00:08:43.889
[S02] What if you're stuck in this rut or you just feel lost?

144
00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:48.419
[S02] What if you're like me, leading up to my birthday, where I was just starting to fall

145
00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:53.220
[S02] off track, but I didn't really notice how it was impacting my life until I finally

146
00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:55.830
[S02] hit that point where everything turned around?

147
00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:57.210
[S02] How do you hit that point?

148
00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:59.460
[S02] And what is the reason behind it working?

149
00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:05.370
[S02] To understand this, we need to understand that evolution creates order from disorder.

150
00:09:05.399 --> 00:09:09.779
[S02] Now, this is an interesting one, because the second law of thermodynamics states that

151
00:09:09.809 --> 00:09:14.580
[S02] all natural processes move toward a state of greater disorder over time.

152
00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:16.889
[S02] In other words, this is what entropy is.

153
00:09:16.919 --> 00:09:20.039
[S02] It's the measure of disorder, randomness, or chaos.

154
00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:25.110
[S02] Now, here's the thing, the universe or your life just moving towards greater disorder

155
00:09:25.139 --> 00:09:28.110
[S02] or randomness or chaos is only half of the story.

156
00:09:28.139 --> 00:09:32.879
[S02] In his book, The End of Uncertainty, Ilya Prigadine makes the argument that this arrow

157
00:09:32.909 --> 00:09:37.440
[S02] of time is not just an illusion, but a fundamental feature of reality itself.

158
00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:42.269
[S02] When a system is pushed far enough away from equilibrium, it can become unstable.

159
00:09:42.299 --> 00:09:47.340
[S02] Now, here's the key point, this instability can lead to a spontaneous emergence of new

160
00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:50.669
[S02] complex ordered patterns called dissipative structures.

161
00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:55.679
[S02] The easiest example to wrap your head around this concept is think of a faucet, and the

162
00:09:55.710 --> 00:09:59.070
[S02] faucet is just pouring out water, and it's very chaotic.

163
00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:04.620
[S02] But at some point, it gets so chaotic that as it's going down the drain, it creates

164
00:10:04.649 --> 00:10:08.669
[S02] this whirlpool, this beautiful ordered structure of a whirlpool.

165
00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:13.919
[S02] That's not more chaotic, that's not more messy or more disorder, that's order that

166
00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:16.169
[S02] has emerged from disorder.

167
00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:20.519
[S02] Now, I know we're getting all scientific or abstract here, but bear with me for a second,

168
00:10:20.539 --> 00:10:23.940
[S02] because there's one more thing that you need to understand, and that's the fact that

169
00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:31.289
[S02] reality is composed of whole parts, not just parts, not just holes, but whole parts.

170
00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:37.110
[S02] An atom is a whole and a part of a molecule, a molecule is a whole and a part of a cell,

171
00:10:37.139 --> 00:10:41.879
[S02] a cell is a part of an organism, an organism a part of an environment, or you can think

172
00:10:41.909 --> 00:10:47.639
[S02] of letter to word to sentence, the paragraph, or matter to life to mind to soul to spirit,

173
00:10:47.669 --> 00:10:53.220
[S02] or even things like the techno-economic base of a society, like foraging to horticultural

174
00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:56.730
[S02] to agrarian to industrial to informational societies.

175
00:10:56.759 --> 00:11:00.570
[S02] Now, the point that I'm trying to get at with illustrating this is that evolution

176
00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:03.179
[S02] creates order from chaos.

177
00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:07.320
[S02] As matter progressed and became more complex, life emerged.

178
00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:09.809
[S02] As life progressed, mind emerged.

179
00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:17.759
[S02] As anything becomes more developed or complex or disordered or messy, a new whole emerges

180
00:11:17.789 --> 00:11:20.879
[S02] as the ordered structure for that thing.

181
00:11:20.909 --> 00:11:23.250
[S02] Now, let's try to tie this all together.

182
00:11:23.269 --> 00:11:28.980
[S02] So when you take the concept of entropy and apply it to the mind, you get psychic entropy.

183
00:11:29.009 --> 00:11:33.539
[S02] In other words, the mind tends toward disorder, randomness, and chaos.

184
00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:37.710
[S02] So in your personal life, when you reach a point of feeling lost, or you feel like

185
00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:43.139
[S02] your life is falling into chaos, you are presented with an opportunity for a higher

186
00:11:43.169 --> 00:11:45.629
[S02] version of yourself to emerge.

187
00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.639
[S03] Actually, I just hit that point. I hit the point of becoming so fed up with the progress that I was making

188
00:12:05.759 --> 00:12:10.320
[S03] that I had no choice but to flip the script and do the complete opposite. The reason

189
00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:15.240
[S03] that many people don't reach this point is because they aren't brutally aware

190
00:12:15.279 --> 00:12:21.840
[S03] of how their current trajectory in life is impacting their thoughts, emotions, and progress toward

191
00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:28.080
[S03] non-existing goals. So you need to reach the crossroads of dissonance and insight.

192
00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:34.470
[S03] What I mean by that is, once you get a taste of where you are in life, you've gotten, you've, you've been there

193
00:12:34.519 --> 00:12:41.679
[S03] for long enough, you will start to feel like you are being ripped in half, and that's exactly what you need to pay attention to.

194
00:12:41.759 --> 00:12:47.440
[S03] One lower part of you wants to stay the same, while one higher part of you wants to change.

195
00:12:47.519 --> 00:12:53.759
[S03] And as you intentionally choose to lean toward the higher, you are met by insight.

196
00:12:53.799 --> 00:12:59.399
[S03] You are given the answer to everything, and that leads to this insane burst of clarity

197
00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:06.000
[S03] that leads to obsession for a meaningful goal. That is the tipping point that pushes you into being extreme.

198
00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:12.360
[S03] Now, once you hit this point, this is when you disappear for six months and focus on four habits.

199
00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:19.039
[S03] New knowledge comes when you simply bear in mind what you need to know. Keep holding the problem in mind, and it will yield.

200
00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:22.240
[S03] The history of human beings is certainly a testament to that fact.

201
00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:27.879
[S03] An individual runs into a problem and simply obsesses about that problem until he or she solves it.

202
00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:32.360
[S03] It might take a week, a month, a year, a decade, a century, or a millennium,

203
00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:36.600
[S03] but the cosmos appears to be such that solutions are always forthcoming.

204
00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:41.559
[S03] That's a quote from Ken Wilber that helps summarize this nicely, is that "all problems are solvable."

205
00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:47.879
[S03] And that's exactly what you're doing when you go into this period of monk mode, or disappearing for six months.

206
00:13:47.919 --> 00:13:52.960
[S03] You're obsessing over a problem. Now, on the topic of monk mode, I'm sure you're familiar with it.

207
00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:57.440
[S03] It's a concept that's often talked about in the self-improvement space, and recently it's just become a

208
00:13:57.519 --> 00:14:02.960
[S03] bastardized version of its former self. Everyone has talked about it, and now since everyone knows about it,

209
00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:08.039
[S03] it's often reduced to this shallow definition of just cut everyone off and make money.

210
00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:13.559
[S03] So we're not gonna do that. In fact, we're not even gonna follow the original definition of what monk mode was.

211
00:14:13.629 --> 00:14:19.720
[S03] What the original monk mode means is a purely ascending lifestyle, or an ascetic lifestyle.

212
00:14:19.799 --> 00:14:26.000
[S03] So, in the past agrarian societies, when the horse drawn plow was invented, there was an abundance of food,

213
00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:29.360
[S03] and so men had the free time to pursue their own interests.

214
00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:34.399
[S03] So this is when things like writing and mathematics were invented, but it also gave people the time

215
00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:40.279
[S03] for deep contemplation. And what they found is that the divine wasn't out there

216
00:14:40.559 --> 00:14:46.200
[S03] or up there, but in here, and that realization led to the axial period.

217
00:14:46.279 --> 00:14:51.279
[S03] And the axial period was a time where revolutionary spiritual and philosophical ideas emerged

218
00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:58.240
[S03] across multiple civilizations. So Socrates in ancient Greece, Gautama Buddha in India, and Lao Tzu in China.

219
00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:03.600
[S03] So this ascending spirituality that was created, or asceticism, is a view

220
00:15:03.679 --> 00:15:10.440
[S03] that pins the manifest world as evil. Since the kingdom of heaven couldn't be found up there or out there,

221
00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:17.519
[S03] money, sex, food, and pleasure were demonized. So true monk mode, the original definition of monk mode, is just this.

222
00:15:17.559 --> 00:15:21.320
[S03] It's a period dedicated to the ascending path and self-realization.

223
00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:26.320
[S03] But the thing is here, we're in 2025. We have access to a lot of information, a lot of different worldviews.

224
00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:32.200
[S03] We have access to history. Any non-dogmatic person realizes that spirituality is only one piece of the puzzle.

225
00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:37.960
[S03] It's a very important piece, but reducing the solution to all of your problems to contemplation

226
00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:44.679
[S03] is just as bad as reducing the same to atoms, money, or just parts or physical things.

227
00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:52.000
[S03] The spiritual people who demonize the scientific materialists for reducing the world to atoms are doing the same thing.

228
00:15:52.039 --> 00:15:58.159
[S03] They're just reducing the world to spirit. So, instead of doing that, we're going to disappear for a period of intensity

229
00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:02.759
[S03] toward a better life. A period where you obsess over a problem until it's solved.

230
00:16:02.799 --> 00:16:09.679
[S03] And our problem here is that we want to change the entirety of our life. We need a holistic monk mode, if you will.

231
00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:14.519
[S03] Now, that comes in two parts: defense and offense. So we'll start with defense,

232
00:16:14.679 --> 00:16:18.679
[S03] which is short and to the point, which is eliminating distractions.

233
00:16:18.759 --> 00:16:25.440
[S03] Because focus does not only apply to work. Distractions are not only notifications that pull you away from work.

234
00:16:25.519 --> 00:16:31.950
[S03] The entire point of disappearing is to create a glitch in the matrix, to change your identity overnight,

235
00:16:32.039 --> 00:16:36.559
[S03] to rid yourself of the crabs and parasites that pull you down and drain you of the energy

236
00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:39.799
[S03] that must be dedicated to the choices that create a better future.

237
00:16:39.879 --> 00:16:46.000
[S03] Now, for the sake of being extreme, it's easier to change everything at once than it is to change one thing at a time.

238
00:16:46.039 --> 00:16:51.559
[S03] So rip the Band-Aid off. Stop responding to people who only take and never give.

239
00:16:51.639 --> 00:16:55.879
[S03] Any invitation that pulls you away from your goals should be an automatic and firm no.

240
00:16:55.950 --> 00:17:00.039
[S03] Every bad habit that does not serve you stops tomorrow. No exceptions.

241
00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:06.799
[S03] It is an incredible feeling to choose your own limitations, to be free of that which splits your attention

242
00:17:06.829 --> 00:17:13.440
[S03] from the highest good you can do. Now, that's defense. Now, for offense, we need to form four focus habits.

243
00:17:13.519 --> 00:17:18.200
[S03] Because a good life stems from the pursuit of the good, the true, and the beautiful.

244
00:17:18.319 --> 00:17:24.920
[S03] The pursuit of truth as a whole. The good is inner subjective truth, what is good for humanity or community.

245
00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:29.799
[S03] It's doing what's right. The true is objective truth, which is found in scientific study.

246
00:17:29.839 --> 00:17:36.160
[S03] And science derives data. Data is experience, and experience is physical, mental, and spiritual.

247
00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:39.519
[S03] The true is about learning and understanding how things really are.

248
00:17:39.599 --> 00:17:45.759
[S03] Now, the beautiful is subjective truth, the realm of aesthetics, noticing and creating beauty around you.

249
00:17:45.839 --> 00:17:50.960
[S03] Now, there are many variations of habits that can check all of these boxes, but we're going to focus on four.

250
00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:54.960
[S03] The four that I think are the most impactful and easiest to focus on.

251
00:17:55.039 --> 00:17:59.799
[S03] So, what you're going to disappear and do for the next six months is this.

252
00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:03.839
[S04] One project, one book, one meditation, one workout.

253
00:18:03.869 --> 00:18:08.549
[S04] That checks the boxes of the good, the true and the beautiful and each dimension of your life.

254
00:18:08.609 --> 00:18:12.599
[S04] So let's start with one project, the thing that you're going to build.

255
00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:15.450
[S04] Your project is the thing you're going to work on.

256
00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:21.029
[S04] It's your work because work is a necessary part of life that brings contrast to rest.

257
00:18:21.059 --> 00:18:25.680
[S04] But most people work jobs they hate, so they throw the baby out with the bathwater,

258
00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:28.259
[S04] hoping that they will never have to work again.

259
00:18:28.289 --> 00:18:32.670
[S04] This is a delusion because you will not be happy in a state of perpetual vacation

260
00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:34.769
[S04] because meaning is found in contrast.

261
00:18:34.799 --> 00:18:38.670
[S04] If you don't work to balance out rest, then the rest becomes meaningless.

262
00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:42.180
[S04] You go on vacation and after a month you want to go back home, you want to get back to work.

263
00:18:42.210 --> 00:18:44.940
[S04] The problem is you're working on the wrong thing.

264
00:18:44.970 --> 00:18:51.119
[S04] And on top of that, the average person's view of what rest is, where rest is recovery.

265
00:18:51.150 --> 00:18:54.359
[S04] Most people view it as this form of self-destruction.

266
00:18:54.390 --> 00:18:59.339
[S04] You lay in bed, you get fat, you eat Cheetos, you drink wine, you become a degenerate.

267
00:18:59.369 --> 00:19:03.569
[S04] That's not rest, that's just a recipe for burnout because you're not recovering

268
00:19:03.599 --> 00:19:05.190
[S04] for any form of meaningful work.

269
00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:10.380
[S04] So the start of the solution is to choose one project that acts as a building block

270
00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:12.059
[S04] for the life you want to live.

271
00:19:12.089 --> 00:19:15.329
[S04] Now, this can be anything, a creative project that leads to independent work,

272
00:19:15.359 --> 00:19:17.940
[S04] a portfolio project that leads to a change in career.

273
00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:20.670
[S04] And most of the time, considering the state of AI,

274
00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:24.240
[S04] I at least recommend that everyone try entrepreneurship.

275
00:19:24.269 --> 00:19:30.599
[S04] Because I know many people don't want to do that, but the way you hedge yourself against replacement is that.

276
00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:36.240
[S04] And because you are attempting to control the good, the true and the beautiful in your work.

277
00:19:36.269 --> 00:19:39.480
[S04] You can't control that when someone else assigns you the work.

278
00:19:39.509 --> 00:19:43.799
[S04] Your work and the project that moves you toward creating the work you want

279
00:19:43.829 --> 00:19:46.529
[S04] is a representation of the good.

280
00:19:46.559 --> 00:19:50.730
[S04] Because entrepreneurship is the art of solving problems in other people's lives.

281
00:19:50.759 --> 00:19:54.839
[S04] It is how you contribute to a community or an audience of people

282
00:19:54.869 --> 00:19:57.210
[S04] by fulfilling a role that improves their lives.

283
00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:02.279
[S04] Now, obviously, there is the bad side of work, which is doing work for someone else

284
00:20:02.309 --> 00:20:06.210
[S04] and being completely unconscious of the impact that that work is having on society

285
00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:08.940
[S04] or culture or the community or people as a whole.

286
00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:12.029
[S04] Some people are working some random desk job and don't even care

287
00:20:12.059 --> 00:20:16.130
[S04] or don't even notice that their company is in coordination with another one

288
00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:18.569
[S04] that drops bombs in another country.

289
00:20:18.599 --> 00:20:23.039
[S04] Your work also integrates the true because you have to learn

290
00:20:23.069 --> 00:20:26.549
[S04] and actually try to get objective results for other people.

291
00:20:26.579 --> 00:20:30.210
[S04] And it integrates the beautiful by expressing the depths of your personality.

292
00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:33.960
[S04] Because the best type of business or brand is one with personality,

293
00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:39.410
[S04] either as one person, as a personal brand, or as a business with personality.

294
00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:44.460
[S04] So spend one hour of deep work a day on a project that can change your professional life.

295
00:20:44.759 --> 00:20:46.049
[S04] Put it on your calendar.

296
00:20:46.079 --> 00:20:49.680
[S04] Work that doesn't feel like work is the intersection of what the world needs,

297
00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:54.630
[S04] the good, what you have experience with, the true, and what you deeply care about, the beautiful.

298
00:20:54.660 --> 00:20:58.019
[S04] Work, when done right, is synonymous with life.

299
00:20:58.049 --> 00:21:01.049
[S04] It is the central pillar of everything else you do.

300
00:21:01.079 --> 00:21:04.769
[S04] And that leads into number two, which is one book.

301
00:21:04.799 --> 00:21:08.099
[S04] Now, you're bound to finish one book in six to 12 months.

302
00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:12.210
[S04] You can read more, but it just didn't make sense for me to say two books or three books

303
00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:15.750
[S04] when we're saying one project, one book, one meditation, one workout.

304
00:21:15.779 --> 00:21:16.529
[S04] It just didn't make sense.

305
00:21:16.559 --> 00:21:20.549
[S04] But if you want to change your life, you need the knowledge to do so, plain and simple.

306
00:21:20.579 --> 00:21:24.059
[S04] And school probably isn't going to give you that knowledge, so you need to self-educate.

307
00:21:24.269 --> 00:21:29.099
[S04] You need to immerse your mind in the thoughts of those who have lived the life you want to live.

308
00:21:29.130 --> 00:21:30.269
[S04] Now, this is simple enough.

309
00:21:30.509 --> 00:21:32.460
[S04] Read one book, 30 minutes a day.

310
00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:38.279
[S04] Because learning something, just because you're curious, is a prime representation of the true.

311
00:21:38.309 --> 00:21:43.319
[S04] And as you'll find, the simple act of exploration brings novel ideas that lead to progress.

312
00:21:43.349 --> 00:21:46.230
[S04] Now, the third habit is one meditation.

313
00:21:46.259 --> 00:21:48.809
[S04] While work can be beautiful and spiritual,

314
00:21:48.839 --> 00:21:53.880
[S04] it is not a full replacement for a habit that maintains your connection with reality.

315
00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:57.569
[S04] Now, by meditation, I do not mean sitting in a dark room

316
00:21:57.599 --> 00:22:00.839
[S04] and closing your eyes and focusing on your breath and letting your thoughts pass,

317
00:22:00.869 --> 00:22:02.670
[S04] even though that's very viable, you can do that.

318
00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:05.970
[S04] Meditation, for all I'm concerned, is a way of life,

319
00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:09.660
[S04] a way of perceiving the world through a lens of wonder and gratitude,

320
00:22:09.690 --> 00:22:13.349
[S04] noticing the amazing things all around you that tend to be glanced over

321
00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:15.420
[S04] when you are stressed and narrow-minded.

322
00:22:15.660 --> 00:22:19.319
[S04] The purpose of meditation in this sense is to notice depth,

323
00:22:19.349 --> 00:22:22.109
[S04] to escape the trap of shallow living.

324
00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:28.230
[S04] So for at least 10 minutes a day, attempt to notice reality in greater detail and depth.

325
00:22:28.259 --> 00:22:31.769
[S04] Go on a walk and only focus on the feeling of your feet on the ground.

326
00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:35.730
[S04] Stare at a tree and notice the intricate detail that you've never noticed before.

327
00:22:35.759 --> 00:22:38.670
[S04] Listen deeply when having a conversation with a loved one.

328
00:22:38.819 --> 00:22:42.720
[S04] Don't just notice words, notice tone, expression, everything.

329
00:22:42.750 --> 00:22:44.819
[S04] Stare at your hand for 10 minutes straight.

330
00:22:44.849 --> 00:22:45.869
[S04] Yes, I'm serious.

331
00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:48.660
[S04] And let your mind wander deeper into the detail.

332
00:22:48.690 --> 00:22:51.150
[S04] Feel the water on your hands while doing dishes.

333
00:22:51.180 --> 00:22:53.730
[S04] Feel everything you touch for once in your life.

334
00:22:53.759 --> 00:22:56.519
[S04] Listen to the sound of silence or cars in the distance.

335
00:22:56.549 --> 00:22:59.730
[S04] Pick apart the intricate smell of even the most boring food.

336
00:22:59.759 --> 00:23:04.349
[S04] Don't overcomplicate this, and this leads into the last and final habit, which is one workout.

337
00:23:04.380 --> 00:23:06.829
[S04] And I'll let Socrates tee this one off.

338
00:23:06.859 --> 00:23:10.200
[S04] No man has the right to be an amateur in the matter of physical training.

339
00:23:10.230 --> 00:23:13.859
[S04] It is a shame for a man to grow old without seeing the beauty and strength

340
00:23:13.890 --> 00:23:15.390
[S04] of which his body is capable.

341
00:23:15.509 --> 00:23:19.319
[S04] Now, back to the topic of reality being composed of whole parts.

342
00:23:19.349 --> 00:23:24.119
[S04] One major tenet of these whole parts is that the destruction of the lower

343
00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:04.130
[S05] lack of health shoots its impact upward into the creative ability of your mind,

344
00:24:04.160 --> 00:24:07.730
[S05] the quality of your work, and the depth of connections in your relationships.

345
00:24:07.759 --> 00:24:12.759
[S05] It's all connected. So, commit to one workout, whatever you feel the most pulled to do.

346
00:24:12.799 --> 00:24:17.000
[S05] Find a running program, a weightlifting program, a Pilates routine, a yoga thing, whatever.

347
00:24:17.029 --> 00:24:21.930
[S05] The point is to pay mind to the body that allows you to be here every single day.

348
00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:25.059
[S05] Those four habits can radically change your life.

349
00:24:25.099 --> 00:24:29.259
[S05] Those four habits can align you with the good, the true, and the beautiful.

350
00:24:29.299 --> 00:24:34.529
[S05] Those four habits can lead to a higher state of enjoyment through curiosity, passion,

351
00:24:34.559 --> 00:24:36.759
[S05] purpose, autonomy, and mastery.

352
00:24:36.799 --> 00:24:40.930
[S05] Get sick of being sick. Give yourself permission to be extreme.

353
00:24:40.960 --> 00:24:43.829
[S05] Disappear and come back unrecognizable.

354
00:24:43.859 --> 00:24:47.730
[S05] Thank you for watching this video. If you want to read my latest letter

355
00:24:47.759 --> 00:24:52.059
[S05] on the death of thoughtful creation, how to get ahead of everyone else going into the future,

356
00:24:52.099 --> 00:24:54.099
[S05] you can read that on my Substack for free.

357
00:24:54.130 --> 00:24:57.829
[S05] And on the paid version of the Substack, I actually released the full course

358
00:24:57.859 --> 00:24:59.559
[S05] of the One Person Business Launchpad.

359
00:24:59.599 --> 00:25:03.400
[S05] So you can go to my Substack, check out What's All There, sign up for free or paid.

360
00:25:03.430 --> 00:25:06.029
[S05] That's it for this video. Thank you for watching. Bye.
